<TopicPage slug="subjects/anthropology/advanced/ethnographic-methods">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    How can anyone know another way of life — not its surface, but what it feels like to live in
    it? Ethnography is anthropology's answer: <strong>sustained immersion until the strange becomes
    intelligible</strong>. Its evidentiary wager is that meaning is invisible to instruments and
    inaccessible to surveys — people cannot report their own taken-for-granted world any more than a
    fish can report water — so the only route is to live inside it long enough for the
    taken-for-granted to surface. The method costs scale (one community studied deeply, not
    thousands sampled shallowly) and buys a kind of validity no other social method claims:
    texture, process, causation-in-context.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the method, the contract, the lenses, the ethics" widget="equationStep" config={ethnCfg} />

  <h2>Malinowski's revolution: the fieldwork standard</h2>
  <p>
    Before 1914, anthropology was armchair work — scholars synthesising reports from traders,
    missionaries and colonial officers, separated from their data by oceans. Bronisław Malinowski,
    stranded in the Trobriand Islands by the First World War, set the standard modern fieldwork has
    followed since: live in the midst of the people studied, learn the language, participate in
    daily life, and record what he called the "imponderabilia of actual life" — the routines no one
    thinks to tell an outsider because no one thinks of them at all. <em>Argonauts of the Western
    Pacific</em> (1922) demonstrated the payoff: the Kula ring, a ceremonial exchange system
    circulating shell valuables across islands in patterns no questionnaire would ever have
    surfaced. Its two lessons still define the craft: immersion is not decoration but evidence
    (meaning lives in context), and the ethnographer's body in the field is the instrument —
    which makes the instrument's calibration (language, relationships, position) a methodological
    problem in itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Participant observation in practice</h2>
  <p>
    The method is a paradox: observe by participating, participate by observing. Its working
    practices are exacting. <strong>Fieldnotes</strong> are the discipline's backbone — written
    daily, ideally in two registers: dense descriptive notes (what happened, verbatim where possible)
    and analytic memos (hunches, patterns, questions), kept separate so observation and
    interpretation do not contaminate each other at the source. <strong>Key informants</strong> —
    individuals who take on the ethnographer's education — are half the method and half its risk:
    every key informant is a perspective, not a window; the craft requires many and their
    disagreement. The conventional <strong>duration of a year or more</strong> is not ritual: it
    captures a full annual cycle (seasons, rituals, harvests, budget cycles — the rhythms of
    anything) and, just as importantly, outlasts the initial performance of the guest role, after
    which ordinary, unrehearsed life becomes observable. Planning a limited fieldwork project runs
    the same logic compressed: pre-fieldwork language and negotiation, entry (who brokers access
    shapes what you can see), sustained presence, and a written report honest about its own limits.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "ethnography is just hanging out and writing it up." The
    immersion is instrumented: structured observation protocols, interview schedules, life
    histories, genealogies, spatial mapping, sometimes surveys and archival work — all triangulated.
    The best fieldwork produces data whose provenance, coverage and biases are documented as
    carefully as any laboratory's.
  </p>

  <h2>Data collection: the toolkit inside the immersion</h2>
  <p>
    Participant observation anchors a battery of techniques, each adding a register.
    <strong>Interviewing</strong> runs from informal conversation through life histories (biography
    as cultural document) to semi-structured protocols; <strong>genealogical method</strong> (from
    Rivers onward) maps kinship — for decades the skeleton key to social organisation; <strong>social
    network mapping</strong> brings the sociology lesson's measures into the village; <strong>mapping
    and material inventories</strong> record space and things; participation in economic activities
    (working the harvest, keeping accounts alongside households) yields economic data no interview
    retrieves honestly. The qualitative/quantitative division the syllabus names is a division of
    labour, not of rank: numbers answer how much, ethnography answers what the numbers <em>are</em>
    — why the attendance figure falls after the ritual calendar shifts, why the price index misses
    the reciprocity economy. Development anthropology's hardest-learned lesson is that projects
    designed on survey data alone fail on meanings; projects designed with local categories in the
    loop survive contact with reality.
  </p>

  <h2>Emic, etic, and the analysis of meaning</h2>
  <p>
    The classic epistemological pair: <strong>emic</strong> — the insider's categories and causes
    ("the ancestors are angry"), <strong>etic</strong> — the analyst's comparative frame ("this
    ritual redistributes wealth and enforces reciprocity networks"). Neither suffices alone; the
    discipline's art is moving between them without collapsing one into the other. Geertz's
    <strong>thick description</strong> gave the interpretive program its charter: the difference
    between a twitch and a wink is not muscular but semiotic, and ethnography's object is exactly
    that difference — layered significance recoverable only in context. Analysis in practice runs
    through coding (marking fieldnotes for themes), constant comparison (theme against theme,
    case against case — grounded theory's contribution), negative-case analysis (hunting what
    <em>breaks</em> your emerging account, the qualitative analogue of falsification), and finally
    the written research report — which is not a container for findings but the analysis itself:
    the genre conventions of ethnographic writing became a subject of study in their own right
    (the 1980s <em>Writing Culture</em> turn), because a text written in one voice, with one
    authority, silently manufactures an omniscience the field never had.
  </p>

  <h2>Reflexivity: the observer in the instrument</h2>
  <p>
    Since the ethnographer <em>is</em> the instrument, the instrument's properties must be reported.
    <strong>Reflexivity</strong> — documenting how your nationality, gender, class, race, language
    and institutional position shaped what you could see, whom you could approach, and what you
    took for granted — matured from confession into method: reflexive accounts are more evaluable,
    not less. <strong>Positionality</strong> is its practical face: your presence changes the field
    (the observer effect at human scale — informants perform for the guest, at least at first), and
    different positions open different doors (a woman researcher reaches spaces closed to men and
    vice versa; the craft deliberately uses this, recording which vantage each datum came from).
    The honest standard is not neutrality but transparency: no one escapes a standpoint; the
    accountable researcher maps theirs and shows their work.
  </p>

  <h2>Ethics: from subjects to partners</h2>
  <p>
    Anthropology's ethical trajectory tracks its political history. The discipline grew inside
    colonial administration (Malinowski's own field was a colonial dependency), and the reckoning
    ran through documented crises — most explosively the <strong>Yanomami controversy</strong>
    (Tierney's 2000 <em>Darkness in El Dorado</em> alleged ethical and physical harms in decades of
    research; the firestorm, whatever its specific charges' accuracy, permanently changed
    governance: pre-fieldwork review, informed consent <em>in forms communities can actually
    exercise</em>, anonymity and pseudonymity with care (small populations are identifiable
    precisely because they are small), reciprocity (research owes something back), data
    sovereignty (who owns fieldnotes, recordings, genetic samples — the post-genomics frontier),
    and the right of communities to review or refuse research about them. Fieldwork in conflict
    zones, with vulnerable populations, or under repressive states adds the dual-obligation
    problems (protecting informants versus the law; the encrypted-notes standard exists because
    fieldnotes have been subpoenaed and seized). The modern codes — AAA, ASA, the various national
    bodies — converge on one principle: research <em>with</em>, not <em>on</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>Interviewing: the conversation as instrument</h2>
  <p>
    Interviewing within ethnography is not surveying by mouth. <strong>Unstructured and
    conversational interviews</strong> follow the informant's logic; <strong>semi-structured
    protocols</strong> hold a topic guide loosely, allowing paths the researcher did not
    imagine; <strong>life histories</strong> treat biography as cultural document — what a
    life course reveals about the social world that shaped it. Craft matters: open questions
    before closed; silence as tool (the second answer, after the pause, is often the real
    one); example-chasing (ask for the specific episode rather than the general belief —
    "tell me about the last time" beats "do you usually"); and the recognition that
    interviews are performances mutually staged — what a person tells a known outsider is a
    social fact about the relationship, not a window onto private truth. Recording,
    transcription conventions (pauses, overlaps) and translation (the layer where categories
    travel or break) are methodological decisions with analytic consequences. And interviews
    triangulate against observation: where talk and practice diverge — people describing
    ideals they do not enact, or enacting rules they cannot articulate — is precisely where
    the interesting findings live.
  </p>

  <h2>The written research report: writing as analysis</h2>
  <p>
    The report is not packaging; it is the analysis. Genre conventions carry epistemic
    weight: <strong>thick description</strong> (the scene rendered in enough context that a
    stranger can re-derive the meaning), the case narrative that lets structure emerge from
    particulars, dialogue and fieldnote excerpts as evidence, and an analytic voice that
    distinguishes observation from interpretation on the page. The <em>Writing Culture</em>
    critique (1986) made the politics explicit: ethnographic authority was constructed — the
    all-seeing fieldworker voice erased dialogue and position — and the discipline's response
    (polyphony, dialogic editing, participant co-authorship) changed practice, unevenly.
    Modern standards: positionality statements that do real work (what access, what blind
    spots), anonymisation handled honestly (pseudonyms and composite details where needed,
    with the special tensions of small, identifiable fields), and reflexivity without
    confessional paralysis. The craft's test: a report another researcher could use — methods
    described precisely enough that the inference from evidence to claim is auditable, which
    is ethnography's version of a methods section a replication could grip.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Multi-sited and digital turns.</strong> Marcus's multi-sited ethnography answered
    globalisation: if your object is a supply chain, a policy circuit or a diaspora, the "field"
    is distributed — follow the thing, the people, the metaphor. Digital ethnography (Boellstorff's
    <em>Coming of Age in Second Life</em> is the methodological exemplar) transferred immersion to
    virtual worlds with the same standards (years of presence, learned vernacular, fieldnotes) and
    new consent problems (is a platform a public place? — the answer reshaped internet research
    ethics). <strong>Validity in qualitative work.</strong> The craft's answer to the replication
    crisis is procedural: Lincoln and Guba's trustworthiness criteria (credibility, transferability,
    dependability, confirmability), triangulation across methods and informants, member checking
    (returning accounts to participants for correction), audit trails, and thick documentation —
    "validity" in ethnography is argued, not computed, and the argument is auditable.
    <strong>The institutional migration.</strong> Ethnographic methods now run inside corporate
    research (Intel, Microsoft, IDEO — observational UX research is fieldwork), medicine (Kleinman's
    explanatory-models work: what the illness <em>is</em> to the patient drives adherence more than
    the pathology does), public health (rapid ethnographic assessment in epidemics — the methods
    that found why Ebola burial practices resisted intervention), and humanitarian response.
    <strong>Open questions:</strong> how consent and anonymity survive persistent surveillance
    platforms and searchable archives (yesterday's pseudonymised village is tomorrow's geotagged
    photo set); how the discipline operationalises decolonisation beyond acknowledgment — data
    repatriation, community co-authorship, Indigenous governance of research; what generative-AI
    transcription and translation do to the interpretive craft (efficiency gained, whose
    categories imposed?); and whether multi-sited, team-based, long-duration ethnography — the
    method the era's problems actually demand — can be funded and trained at the scale the
    century needs.
  </p>
  <p>
    A final craft note: the best ethnographies are disciplined by their questions, not their
    settings. The method earns its authority when the research question genuinely requires
    immersion — meaning, process, tacit knowledge, the view from inside — and the report
    demonstrates that immersion changed the question (what the field taught the researcher is
    part of the finding). Where a survey would have answered the question, immersion is cost
    without inquiry. Method follows question: the discipline's oldest rule, and the one most
    often violated in its name — the craft's own reflexivity, applied to its choices of where
    and how long to stand still.
  </p>

